        ("INTERMEDIATE", "Which statement correctly opens a file for appending so existing content is preserved?", ["out.open('f.txt', std::ios::out)", "out.open('f.txt', std::ios::app)", "out.open('f.txt', std::ios::trunc)", "out.open('f.txt', std::ios::in)"], "B", "std::ios::app opens the file in append mode, keeping existing data and writing at the end."),
        ("INTERMEDIATE", "class String { public: std::string s; String &operator=(const String &o) { s = o.s; return *this; } }; Why does operator= return *this by reference?", ["To return a copy", "To allow the compiler to optimize memory", "It should return void in idiomatic code", "To support chained assignment such as a = b = c"], "D", "Returning *this by reference lets assignments be chained, as in a = b = c, where the result of b = c is assigned to a."),
        ("INTERMEDIATE", "What is the primary purpose of overloading operator<< as a friend function for a user-defined type?", ["To increase performance", "To allow the object to be sorted", "To replace std::cout entirely", "To access private members while supporting std::cout << obj"], "D", "A friend operator<< can read private members and is called with the stream on the left, as in std::cout << obj."),
        ("ADVANCED", "class Vec { public: Vec &operator++() { return *this; } }; Which version of ++ does this define?", ["Postfix", "Prefix", "Both", "Neither"], "B", "The prefix operator++ takes no parameters and returns a reference to the updated object."),
        ("ADVANCED", "class Vec { public: Vec operator++(int) { Vec old = *this; return old; } }; Which version of ++ does this define?", ["Prefix", "Neither", "Postfix", "Compile error"], "C", "The dummy int parameter identifies the postfix operator++, which returns the object's previous value."),
        ("ADVANCED", "std::ofstream out('n.txt', std::ios::out | std::ios::app); Which best describes this open mode?", ["Read only", "Binary mode", "Write with append, preserving existing content", "Truncate the file"], "C", "std::ios::app positions writes at the end, so existing content is preserved while new data is appended."),
